  "verdict": "revise",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Independent search across 10+ query combinations found no competing work on generational depth scoring (Gen0/Gen1/Gen2 ordinal labeling for training corpus decontamination) — the concept is genuinely novel. The closest adjacent work (arXiv:2502.15654, arXiv:2511.17602, arXiv:2410.14259) all operate on binary or benchmark-overlap axes orthogonal to generational provenance depth."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The 16-day critical path is plausible but contains an unverified load-bearing assumption: the '6-8 GPU hour' generation estimate for 200K documents is never anchored to a document length. FineWeb-Edu/C4 documents averaging 1000 tokens would require ~200M tokens of generation; at realistically batched A100 throughput for Qwen3-0.6B (~3000-5000 tok/s), this runs 11-18h per bottleneck step — potentially adding 2-3 days to the sequential critical path and threatening the deadline. At 500 tokens/document the estimate holds; the proposal needs to commit to a specific document length or truncation cutoff to make the timeline defensible."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "The approach is exceptionally well-specified — thinking mode hard switch, corpus size control, dual null-hypothesis with paired t-test, integration architecture (5 scalar features concatenated to DeBERTa CLS → ordinal head), and contingency triggers are all stated. The only opacity is the omission of document length from generation time estimates."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Perfect EMNLP 2026 special theme alignment on synthetic data feedback loops and data governance; GenDepth-Bench is a reusable community resource; the dual null-hypothesis design guarantees a governance finding regardless of outcome; budget-constrained decontamination is a pressing practical problem as web AI content saturation accelerates."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "TIMELINE RISK — Document length unspecified in generation estimates: The '6-8 GPU hours to generate 200K documents with Qwen3-0.6B' claim is unanchored to a document length. FineWeb-Edu/C4 documents commonly run 500-2000 tokens; at 1000 tokens × 200K docs = 200M tokens, batched A100 inference at 3000-5000 tok/s for Qwen3-0.6B yields 11-18 hours per generational step — not 6-8 hours. Over 2 sequential steps this adds 6-20 hours to the critical path, potentially cascading into a 2-3 day delay and threatening the May 25 deadline. Fix: explicitly commit to truncating/capping generated documents at 512 tokens maximum length, which (a) brings generation time within the estimate (200K × 512 tokens = 102M tokens ÷ 3000-5000 tok/s ≈ 6-9h), (b) is scientifically defensible for depth discrimination experiments, and (c) should be stated explicitly in the approach section so reviewers can verify the estimate.",
    "MISSING CITATION — arXiv:2602.10531 'From Collapse to Improvement: Statistical Perspectives on the Evolutionary Dynamics of Iterative Training on Contaminated Sources' (February 2026) directly analyzes iterative training dynamics on contaminated synthetic data and should be cited in the landscape section. It does not perform depth labeling or classification, so it does not threaten novelty, but its omission is likely to be caught by area chairs familiar with the collapse literature. One sentence differentiating it suffices: 'arXiv:2602.10531 analyzes the statistical dynamics of iterative training on contaminated data but does not assign generational depth labels or propose a depth-stratified filtering method.'"
  ],
  "feedback": "Both Round 6 issues are cleanly resolved: the `enable_thinking=False` tokenizer hard switch plus post-hoc `<think>` token scan properly closes the thinking-mode execution risk, and the fixed-total-size conditions (200K documents per condition, varying only Gen0/Gen1/Gen2 composition) properly close the corpus size confound. Two targeted fixes remain before this can pass: (1) Add an explicit document length cap (e.g., 512 tokens maximum per generated document) to the approach section and re-anchor the '6-8 GPU hour' generation estimate against that length — this makes the timeline defensible for reviewers who will check the arithmetic. (2) Cite arXiv:2602.10531 in the landscape section with a one-sentence differentiation (it analyzes collapse dynamics statistically but does not classify text by generational depth). Both fixes require under 30 minutes of writing and no new experiments.",
